[2026-08-11 01:16:10] [agentic_pipeline.py (510)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 333409] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 1.\n\nCurrent node: N2\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N0, N4\nN0 -> N6\nN9 -> terminal\nN1 -> terminal\nN5 -> N8\nN8 -> N7, N1\nN2 -> N3, N5\nN6 -> terminal\nN3 -> N9\nN4 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N3, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N2, Player 1 has two legal moves: to N3 or to N5. We analyze each option considering the game's outcome and the opponent's responses.\n\nOption 1: Move to N3  \n- From N3, the only edge is to N9, which is a terminal node.  \n- So Player 2 must move from N9 to terminal — no legal moves.  \n- Player 2 loses.  \n- This is a winning move for Player 1.\n\nOption 2: Move to N5  \n- From N5, the only edge is to N8.  \n- Now Player 2 is at N8, which has edges to N7 and N1.  \n- Both N7 and N1 lead to non-terminal nodes (N7 → N0 → N6, or N7 → N4; N1 → terminal).  \n- If Player 2 moves to N1: N1 is terminal → Player 1 loses.  \n- If Player 2 moves to N7: then Player 1 can go from N7 to N0 → N6 (terminal), or N7 → N4 (terminal).  \n  - Either way, Player 1 can force a win after Player 2's move.  \n- But crucially, Player 2 can choose N1, which is terminal — so Player 1 would have no move and lose.  \n- Since Player 2 plays optimally, they will choose the move that leads to their win.  \n- From N8, choosing N1 leads to immediate terminal (Player 1 loses).  \n- So Player 2 will choose N1 and win.  \n- Therefore, moving to N5 leads to Player 1's loss.\n\nThus, moving to N3 results in an immediate win — Player 2 has no legal move after that.  \nThis is the only move that guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -1.0, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N2, Player 1 has two legal moves: to N3 or to N5. We analyze each option considering the game's outcome and the opponent's responses.\n\nOption 1: Move to N3  \n- From N3, the only edge is to N9, which is a terminal node.  \n- So Player 2 must move from N9 to terminal — no legal moves.  \n- Player 2 loses.  \n- This is a winning move for Player 1.\n\nOption 2: Move to N5  \n- From N5, the only edge is to N8.  \n- Now Player 2 is at N8, which has edges to N7 and N1.  \n- Both N7 and N1 lead to non-terminal nodes (N7 → N0 → N6, or N7 → N4; N1 → terminal).  \n- If Player 2 moves to N1: N1 is terminal → Player 1 loses.  \n- If Player 2 moves to N7: then Player 1 can go from N7 to N0 → N6 (terminal), or N7 → N4 (terminal).  \n  - Either way, Player 1 can force a win after Player 2's move.  \n- But crucially, Player 2 can choose N1, which is terminal — so Player 1 would have no move and lose.  \n- Since Player 2 plays optimally, they will choose the move that leads to their win.  \n- From N8, choosing N1 leads to immediate terminal (Player 1 loses).  \n- So Player 2 will choose N1 and win.  \n- Therefore, moving to N5 leads to Player 1's loss.\n\nThus, moving to N3 results in an immediate win — Player 2 has no legal move after that.  \nThis is the only move that guarantees a win.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou ar