[2026-08-11 01:35:37] [agentic_pipeline.py (510)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 333409] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 1.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN6 -> terminal\nN9 -> N0, N3\nN1 -> N4, N6\nN8 -> terminal\nN4 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N1\nN2 -> terminal\nN7 -> N8\nN5 -> terminal\nN0 -> N2\n\nLegal moves: N0, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, Player 1 can move to either N0 or N3. We analyze each option considering the game's structure and the goal of maximizing the chance of winning (i.e., forcing the opponent into a losing position).\n\nFirst, consider moving to N0:\n- N0 → N2 → terminal. So the path is N9 → N0 → N2 → game ends. Player 2 has no move after N2 (terminal), so Player 1 wins. This is a winning move.\n\nNow consider moving to N3:\n- N3 → N1\n- From N1, possible moves: N4 or N6\n  - N4 → N5 or N7\n    - N5 → terminal\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal\n  - N6 → terminal\n- So from N3, the game continues and eventually ends at a terminal node. But since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths from N1 eventually lead to terminal nodes, we must determine who makes the last move.\n\nLet’s evaluate the game tree from N3:\n- N9 → N3 → N1\n- From N1, Player 2 chooses:\n  - N1 → N4 → Player 1 can go to N5 or N7\n    - N5 → terminal → Player 1 has no move? No — Player 1 moves to N5, then game ends. So Player 2 cannot move after that. So Player 1 wins.\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal → Player 1 wins.\n  - N1 → N6 → terminal → Player 1 wins.\n\nWait — actually, after N1, Player 2 moves. So:\n- If Player 1 moves to N3, then Player 2 moves from N1 to either N4 or N6.\n  - If Player 2 moves to N6 → terminal → Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses.\n  - If Player 2 moves to N4 → then Player 1 can go to N5 or N7:\n    - N5 → terminal → Player 1 moves, game ends → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal → Player 1 moves, game ends → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n\nSo if Player 2 chooses N6 from N1, Player 1 loses immediately after N3 → N1 → N6.\n\nBut if Player 2 chooses N4, Player 1 can win.\n\nSo from N3 → N1, Player 2 can choose N6 and win (by forcing Player 1 to have no move). So Player 2 can force a win from N3.\n\nThus, if Player 1 moves to N3, Player 2 can respond with N1 → N6 → terminal → Player 1 loses.\n\nTherefore, N3 is a losing move for Player 1.\n\nNow, if Player 1 moves to N0:\n- N0 → N2 → terminal → game ends → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.\n\nThis is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the best choice is N0, which leads directly to a terminal node and forces Player 2 to lose immediately.\n\n<answer>N0</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, Player 1 can move to either N0 or N3. We analyze each option considering the game's structure and the goal of maximizing the chance of winning (i.e., forcing the opponent into a losing position).\n\nFirst, consider moving to N0:\n- N0 → N2 → terminal. So the path is N9 → N0 → N2 → game ends. Player 2 has no move after N2 (terminal), so Player 1 wins. This is a winning move.\n\nNow consider moving to N3:\n- N3 → N1\n- From N1, possible moves: N4 or N6\n  - N4 → N5 or N7\n    - N5 → terminal\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal\n  - N6 → terminal\n- So from N3, the game continues and eventually ends at a terminal node. But since the game ends when a player cannot move, and all paths from N1 eventually lead to terminal nodes, we must determine who makes the last move.\n\nLet’s evaluate the game tree from N3:\n- N9 → N3 → N1\n- From N1, Player 2 chooses:\n  - N1 → N4 → Player 1 can go to N5 or N7\n    - N5 → terminal → Player 1 has no move? No — Player 1 moves to N5, then game ends. So Player 2 cannot move after that. So Player 1 wins.\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal → Player 1 wins.\n  - N1 → N6 → terminal → Player 1 wins.\n\nWait — actually, after N1, Player 2 moves. So:\n- If Player 1 moves to N3, then Player 2 moves from N1 to either N4 or N6.\n  - If Player 2 moves to N6 → terminal → Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses.\n  - If Player 2 moves to N4 → then Player 1 can go to N5 or N7:\n    - N5 → terminal → Player 1 moves, game ends → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n    - N7 → N8 → terminal → Player 1 moves, game ends → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n\nSo if Player 2 chooses N6 from N1, Player 1 loses immediately after N3 → N1 → N6.\n\nBut if Player 2 chooses N4, Player 1 can win.\n\nSo from N3 → N1, Player 2 can choose N6 and win (by forcing Player 1 to have no move). So Player 2 can force a win from N3.\n\nThus, if Player 1 moves to N3, Player 2 can respond with N1 → N6 → terminal → Player 1 loses.\n\nTherefore, N3 is a losing move for Player 1.\n\nNow, if Player 1 moves to N0:\n- N0 → N2 → terminal → game ends → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.\n\nThis is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the best choice is N0, which leads directly to a terminal node and forces Player 2 to lose immediately.\n\n<answer>N0</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 1.\n\nCurrent node: N0\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N4, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N8\nN4 -> N6\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN5 -> N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N4, N5assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N0, Player 1 can move to either N4 or N5. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.\n\nOption 1: Move to N4  \n- From N4, the only move is to N6.  \n- N6 is a terminal node, so Player 2 has no moves and loses.  \n→ This is a winning move for Player 1.\n\nOption 2: Move to N5  \n- From N5, the only move is to N9.  \n- From N9, Player 1 can move to N7 or N8.  \n  - If to N8: N8 is terminal → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n  - If to N7: From N7, Player 1 can go to N2 or N3.  \n    - N3 is terminal → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n    - N2 → N1 (terminal) → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n→ Regardless of path from N9, Player 1 eventually wins.\n\nHowever, we must determine which move gives Player 1 the best outcome — i.e., ensures a win. Both options lead to Player 1 winning. But the question asks to \"choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\"\n\nIn both cases, Player 1 wins. But in the N4 path, the game ends in just two moves (N0 → N4 → N6), and Player 2 has no move after that. In the N5 path, the game may take longer, but still ends with Player 1 winning.\n\nSince both result in a win, we should pick the one that minimizes the opponent's options or ensures a decisive win with fewer risks. However, both are winning. But note: in the N5 path, Player 2 has more options to respond, so Player 1 must be careful.\n\nBut in fact, from N5 → N9, Player 1 chooses next move. From N9, Player 1 can go to N7 or N8. Both lead to terminal nodes or to N2 → N1. So regardless of choice, Player 1 can force a win.\n\nBut here's the key: in the N4 path, after Player 1 moves to N4, Player 2 is forced to go to N6 (only move), and then the game ends — Player 2 has no move and loses immediately. So Player 1 wins in two moves.\n\nIn the N5 path, Player 1 moves to N5, then Player 2 moves to N9, then Player 1 chooses from N9. So Player 1 still wins, but the game takes longer and Player 2 gets a chance to act.\n\nSince both paths lead to Player 1 winning, and the goal is to maximize outcome (i.e., ensure a win), both are valid. But the question says \"choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\"\n\nIn terms of minimizing opponent's options and ensuring a decisive win with fewer moves, N4 is better because it ends the game immediately after Player 1's second move. Player 2 has no move at all after N4 → N6.\n\nThus, N4 is the optimal choice — it leads to a guaranteed win with minimal risk and no back-and-forth.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -1.0, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N0, Player 1 can move to either N4 or N5. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.\n\nOption 1: Move to N4  \n- From N4, the only move is to N6.  \n- N6 is a terminal node, so Player 2 has no moves and loses.  \n→ This is a winning move for Player 1.\n\nOption 2: Move to N5  \n- From N5, the only move is to N9.  \n- From N9, Player 1 can move to N7 or N8.  \n  - If to N8: N8 is terminal → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n  - If to N7: From N7, Player 1 can go to N2 or N3.  \n    - N3 is terminal → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n    - N2 → N1 (terminal) → Player 2 has no move → Player 1 wins.  \n→ Regardless of path from N9, Player 1 eventually wins.\n\nHowever, we must determine which move gives Player 1 the best outcome — i.e., ensures a win. Both options lead to Player 1 winning. But the question asks to \"choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\"\n\nIn both cases, Player 1 wins. But in the N4 path, the game ends in just two moves (N0 → N4 → N6), and Player 2 has no move after that. In the N5 path, the game may take longer, but still ends with Player 1 winning.\n\nSince both result in a win, we should pick the one that minimizes the opponent's options or ensures a decisive win with fewer risks. However, both are winning. But note: in the N5 path, Player 2 has more options to respond, so Player 1 must be careful.\n\nBut in fact, from N5 → N9, Player 1 chooses next move. From N9, Player 1 can go to N7 or N8. Both lead to terminal nodes or to N2 → N1. So regardless of choice, Player 1 can force a win.\n\nBut here's the key: in the N4 path, after Player 1 moves to N4, Player 2 is forced to go to N6 (only move), and then the game ends — Player 2 has no move and loses immediately. So Player 1 wins in two moves.\n\nIn the N5 path, Player 1 moves to N5, then Player 2 moves to N9, then Player 1 chooses from N9. So Player 1 still wins, but the game takes longer and Player 2 gets a chance to act.\n\nSince both paths lead to Player 1 winning, and the goal is to maximize outcome (i.e., ensure a win), both are valid. But the question says \"choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\"\n\nIn terms of minimizing opponent's options and ensuring a decisive win with fewer moves, N4 is better because it ends the game immediately after Player 1's second move. Player 2 has no move at all after N4 → N6.\n\nThus, N4 is the optimal choice — it leads to a guaranteed win with minimal risk and no back-and-forth.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 2.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N4, N5\nN1 -> terminal\nN9 -> N7, N8\nN4 -> N6\nN2 -> N1\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN5 -> N9\nN3 -> terminal\nN8 -> terminal\nN6 -> terminal\n\nLegal moves: N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>We are Player 2, and the current node is N4. The goal is to choose a legal move that maximizes our final game outcome, meaning we want to force a win (i.e., cause the opponent to have no legal move). The game ends when a player cannot move — so we want to move to a node from which the opponent has no legal moves or to a position where the opponent is forced into a losing position.\n\nCurrent node: N4  \nFrom N4, the only outgoing edge is to N6.  \nN6 → terminal (no outgoing edges).  \n\nSo if Player 2 moves from N4 to N6, then Player 1 has no legal move and loses.  \n\nTherefore, moving to N6 immediately ends the game with Player 1 having no move. This is a winning move for Player 2.  \n\nThere are no other legal moves from N4.  \n\nThus, the optimal choice is to move to N6, which forces the opponent to lose immediately. This is the only legal move, and it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": 0.0, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>We are Player 2, and the current node is N4. The goal is to choose a legal move that maximizes our final game outcome, meaning we want to force a win (i.e., cause the opponent to have no legal move). The game ends when a player cannot move — so we want to move to a node from which the opponent has no legal moves or to a position where the opponent is forced into a losing position.\n\nCurrent node: N4  \nFrom N4, the only outgoing edge is to N6.  \nN6 → terminal (no outgoing edges).  \n\nSo if Player 2 moves from N4 to N6, then Player 1 has no legal move and loses.  \n\nTherefore, moving to N6 immediately ends the game with Player 1 having no move. This is a winning move for Player 2.  \n\nThere are no other legal moves from N4.  \n\nThus, the optimal choice is to move to N6, which forces the opponent to lose immediately. This is the only legal move, and it is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Direc