onse": "<reason>The current node is N9. As Player 2, I must respond to Player 1's move. The graph is a directed acyclic graph (DAG), and the game ends when a player cannot move (i.e., reaches a terminal node or has no outgoing edges).\n\nFrom N9, the only outgoing edge is to N1. N1 is a terminal node (no outgoing edges), so if a player moves to N1, they lose immediately because the next player has no move.\n\nSo, if Player 1 starts at N9 and moves to N1, Player 2 loses immediately. Therefore, Player 1 would never make that move — it's a losing move. But wait: the current node is N9, and it's Player 2's turn. That means Player 1 did not just move to N9 — the current node is N9, and it's Player 2's turn to move.\n\nSo, Player 2 must choose a legal move from N9. The only legal move from N9 is to N1. N1 is a terminal node. If Player 2 moves to N1, then Player 1 has no legal move and loses. Therefore, moving to N1 is a winning move for Player 2.\n\nThus, the only legal move from N9 is to N1, and doing so ends the game immediately with Player 1 losing. This is the only possible move, and it is optimal.\n\nTherefore, the best (and only) move is to N1.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": 0.0, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>The current node is N9. As Player 2, I must respond to Player 1's move. The graph is a directed acyclic graph (DAG), and the game ends when a player cannot move (i.e., reaches a terminal node or has no outgoing edges).\n\nFrom N9, the only outgoing edge is to N1. N1 is a terminal node (no outgoing edges), so if a player moves to N1, they lose immediately because the next player has no move.\n\nSo, if Player 1 starts at N9 and moves to N1, Player 2 loses immediately. Therefore, Player 1 would never make that move — it's a losing move. But wait: the current node is N9, and it's Player 2's turn. That means Player 1 did not just move to N9 — the current node is N9, and it's Player 2's turn to move.\n\nSo, Player 2 must choose a legal move from N9. The 