r who moves to N8 wins → so N4 is winning\n- N0 → to N3, N9 → N9 is terminal → so player can win → N0 is winning\n- N3 → to N2, N4, N6 → all terminal → so player wins → N3 is winning\n- N1 → to N9, N7 → N9 is terminal → so player can win → N1 is winning\n- N6 → terminal → winning\n- N7 → to N4, N0 → both are winning positions for the opponent → so opponent wins from either → so N7 is a **losing** position\n- N5 → to N7 or N1\n\nNow evaluate N5:\n\n- Move to N7: N7 is a **losing** position for the opponent → so if Player 1 moves to N7, then Player 2 is in a losing position → Player 1 wins.\n- Move to N1: N1 is a **winning** position for the opponent → so Player 2 can then win from there.\n\nTherefore, moving to N7 leads to a losing position for the opponent → it is the best choice.\n\nSo Player 1 should move to N7.\n\nFinal outcome: Player 1 wins by moving to N7.\n\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, Player 1 has two legal moves: to N7 or to N1. We must evaluate which move leads to a winning position, considering the structure of the graph and the fact that the player who cannot move loses (i.e., reaches a terminal node).\n\nFirst, analyze terminal nodes: any move to N2, N8, or N9 ends the game immediately. The player who makes the move to a terminal node wins, because the opponent has no legal move afterward.\n\nLet’s evaluate the game from the end backward using game theory (winning/losing positions):\n\n- A node is a **losing position** if all moves from it lead to winning positions for the opponent.\n- A node is a **winning position** if there exists at least one move to a losing position for the opponent.\n\nTerminal nodes (N2, N8, N9) are **losing** for the player who just moved — because the game ends and the opponent has no move. But actually, when you move to a terminal node, you win — so the player who moves to a terminal node wins. So terminal nodes are **winning** for the player who just moved.\n\nWait: C