ext player, then the current player wins.\n\nClarify:\n\n- A node is a losing position if every move from it leads to a winning position for the opponent.\n- A node is a winning position if there exists a move to a losing position for the opponent.\n\nSo:\n- N4 → moves to N5 and N2.\n- N5 is a winning position for the opponent → they can win.\n- N2 is a winning position for the opponent → they can win.\n→ So all moves from N4 lead to winning positions for the opponent → so N4 is a losing position.\n\nTherefore, if Player 1 moves to N4, the opponent is now at a losing position — so Player 1 wins.\n\nIf Player 1 moves to N5:\n- Opponent is at N5 → which is a winning position → opponent can move to N9 (terminal) and force Player 1 to lose → so opponent wins.\n\nTherefore, moving to N5 leads to opponent winning.\n\nThus, the optimal move is to N4.\n\nFinal decision: Player 1 moves from N1 to N4.\n\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, Player 1 can move to either N5 or N4. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position, considering the structure of the graph and the goal of forcing the opponent into a losing position (i.e., a node with no outgoing edges or where all moves lead to a terminal state).\n\nFirst, consider the terminal nodes: N8, N9, N0, N3 — these are terminal and have no outgoing edges. Any player who moves to one of these loses, because the next player has no legal move.\n\nSo, the game ends when a player moves to a terminal node, and the player who makes that move loses.\n\nWe analyze the game backwards using backward induction.\n\nKey observations:\n- Nodes leading to terminal nodes (like N3, N8, N9, N0) are losing positions if the opponent is forced to move from there — but actually, the player who moves to a terminal node loses. So, if a player is at a node with only moves to terminal nodes, then that player will lose if the opponent plays optimally.\n\nLet’s classify nodes as winn