[2026-08-11 02:14:54] [agentic_pipeline.py (510)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 333409] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 1.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN3 -> terminal\nN2 -> N9, N0, N8\nN4 -> N10, N9\nN12 -> terminal\nN7 -> N13\nN6 -> terminal\nN13 -> terminal\nN9 -> N1, N5, N8\nN10 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8 -> N5, N11\nN0 -> N3\nN1 -> N6, N7\nN11 -> terminal\nN5 -> N12\n\nLegal moves: N10, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>The current node is N4. Player 1 must choose a legal move from N4, which has outgoing edges to N10 and N9.\n\nWe analyze both options using backward induction, considering the game is impartial and ends when a player cannot move (i.e., reaches a terminal node or a node with no outgoing edges).\n\nFirst, note terminal nodes (no outgoing edges):  \n- N3, N12, N6, N11, N13 are terminal.  \nThus, any move that leads to one of these ends the game immediately.\n\nWe evaluate the game state from the end backward.\n\n### Step 1: Evaluate terminal nodes\n- If a player moves to a terminal node, the opponent has no move and loses. So, moving to a terminal node is a winning move.\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate nodes with outgoing edges\n\nLet’s evaluate each node from the end.\n\n#### Node N13 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N12 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N6 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N11 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N3 → terminal → losing for next player\n\nSo, any node that leads directly to a terminal node is a winning move if the current player can force that.\n\nNow, evaluate nodes with moves:\n\n#### N9 → N1, N5, N8\n- N1 → N6, N7 → N6 (terminal), N7 → N13 (terminal) → both terminal\n- N5 → N12 (terminal)\n- N8 → N5, N11 (terminal)\n\nSo from N9, all moves lead to either terminal or to a node that eventually leads to terminal.\n\nThus, from N9, any move leads to a terminal node or to a node with only terminal paths. So, if a player moves to N9, the opponent will be forced into a position where they must move to a terminal node — so the opponent loses.\n\nWait: Let's check:\n- N9 → N1 → leads to N6 (terminal) or N7 → N13 (terminal) → both terminal\n- N9 → N5 → N12 (terminal)\n- N9 → N8 → N5 → N12 or N11 (terminal)\n\nSo from N9, all moves lead to a terminal node in one step or two steps. So whoever moves to N9 forces the opponent into a position where they must move to a terminal node — so the opponent loses.\n\nTherefore, **N9 is a winning move**.\n\n#### N10 → N0, N2, N6\n- N0 → N3 (terminal)\n- N2 → N9, N0, N8\n- N6 → terminal\n\nSo:\n- N10 → N0 → N3 (terminal) → opponent moves to terminal → player loses?\nWait: Let's be careful.\n\nIf Player 1 moves to N10, then Player 2 must move from N10 to one of N0, N2, N6.\n\n- If Player 2 moves to N0 → then Player 1 moves to N3 (terminal) → Player 2 loses → so Player 1 wins\n- If Player 2 moves to N6 → terminal → Player 1 loses immediately → bad\n- If Player 2 moves to N2 → then Player 1 can respond from N2 to N9 → which is a winning move (as above)\n\nSo from N10, Player 2 can choose to go to N6 (terminal) → then Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses.\n\nSo if Player 1 moves to N10, Player 2 can choose to go to N6 and win.\n\nThus, **N10 is a losing move** for Player 1 — because Player 2 can force a win.\n\n#### N9 → as shown, all moves lead to terminal nodes in one or two steps — so Player 2 is forced into a terminal move.\n\nSo from N9, no matter what Player 2 does, they end up moving to a terminal node (N1, N5, N8), and then Player 1 wins.\n\nTherefore, **N9 is a winning move**.\n\nThus, Player 1 should choose **N9** to force a win.\n\nFinal decision: Move from N4 to N9.\n\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>The current node is N4. Player 1 must choose a legal move from N4, which has outgoing edges to N10 and N9.\n\nWe analyze both options using backward induction, considering the game is impartial and ends when a player cannot move (i.e., reaches a terminal node or a node with no outgoing edges).\n\nFirst, note terminal nodes (no outgoing edges):  \n- N3, N12, N6, N11, N13 are terminal.  \nThus, any move that leads to one of these ends the game immediately.\n\nWe evaluate the game state from the end backward.\n\n### Step 1: Evaluate terminal nodes\n- If a player moves to a terminal node, the opponent has no move and loses. So, moving to a terminal node is a winning move.\n\n### Step 2: Evaluate nodes with outgoing edges\n\nLet’s evaluate each node from the end.\n\n#### Node N13 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N12 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N6 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N11 → terminal → losing for next player\n#### Node N3 → terminal → losing for next player\n\nSo, any node that leads directly to a terminal node is a winning move if the current player can force that.\n\nNow, evaluate nodes with moves:\n\n#### N9 → N1, N5, N8\n- N1 → N6, N7 → N6 (terminal), N7 → N13 (terminal) → both terminal\n- N5 → N12 (terminal)\n- N8 → N5, N11 (terminal)\n\nSo from N9, all moves lead to either terminal or to a node that eventually leads to terminal.\n\nThus, from N9, any move leads to a terminal node or to a node with only terminal paths. So, if a player moves to N9, the opponent will be forced into a position where they must move to a terminal node — so the opponent loses.\n\nWait: Let's check:\n- N9 → N1 → leads to N6 (terminal) or N7 → N13 (terminal) → both terminal\n- N9 → N5 → N12 (terminal)\n- N9 → N8 → N5 → N12 or N11 (terminal)\n\nSo from N9, all moves lead to a terminal node in one step or two steps. So whoever moves to N9 forces the opponent into a position where they must move to a terminal node — so the opponent loses.\n\nTherefore, **N9 is a winning move**.\n\n#### N10 → N0, N2, N6\n- N0 → N3 (terminal)\n- N2 → N9, N0, N8\n- N6 → terminal\n\nSo:\n- N10 → N0 → N3 (terminal) → opponent moves to terminal → player loses?\nWait: Let's be careful.\n\nIf Player 1 moves to N10, then Player 2 must move from N10 to one of N0, N2, N6.\n\n- If Player 2 moves to N0 → then Player 1 moves to N3 (terminal) → Player 2 loses → so Player 1 wins\n- If Player 2 moves to N6 → terminal → Player 1 loses immediately → bad\n- If Player 2 moves to N2 → then Player 1 can respond from N2 to N9 → which is a winning move (as above)\n\nSo from N10, Player 2 can choose to go to N6 (terminal) → then Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses.\n\nSo if Player 1 moves to N10, Player 2 can choose to go to N6 and win.\n\nThus, **N10 is a losing move** for Player 1 — because Player 2 can force a win.\n\n#### N9 → as shown, all moves lead to terminal nodes in one or two steps — so Player 2 is forced into a terminal move.\n\nSo from N9, no matter what Player 2 does, they end up moving to a terminal node (N1, N5, N8), and then Player 1 wins.\n\nTherefore, **N9 is a winning move**.\n\nThus, Player 1 should choose **N9** to force a win.\n\nFinal decision: Move from N4 to N9.\n\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the opponent's possible responses before choosing.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else.\n\nYou are Player 1.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN0 -> N4\nN10 -> terminal\n