e → Player 1 loses → Player 2 wins → bad for Player 1.\n\nAh! This is critical. I made a mistake earlier.\n\nLet’s re-analyze carefully.\n\nAfter Player 1 moves to N9, Player 2 can choose:\n- N6 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses → Player 2 wins → bad\n- N3 → leads to N11 or N13\n  - N11 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses → Player 2 wins\n  - N13 → leads to N5, N12, N2\n    - N5 → N7 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 loses\n    - N12 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 loses\n    - N2 → N0 → N4 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 loses\nSo from N3, any move leads to terminal → Player 2 wins\n- N8 → N10 → terminal → game ends → Player 1 loses\n\nThus, from N9, **every move leads to Player 1 losing** → Player 2 wins.\n\nSo N1 → N9 leads to Player 2 winning → bad for Player 1.\n\nNow Option 2: N1 → N2  \nN2 → N0 → N4 → terminal → game ends → Player 2 has no move → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n\nThus, Player 1 wins immediately.\n\nTherefore, the only move that leads to a win is N1 → N2.\n\nEven though N2 leads to N0 → N4, which is terminal, the game ends after Player 2's turn — so Player 2 cannot move after that.\n\nSo after Player 1 moves to N2, Player 2 must move from N2 to N0. Then Player 1 moves from N0 to N4 (terminal), and now Player 2 has no move — so Player 2 loses.\n\nWait — no. After N1 → N2, Player 2 is at N2. From N2, only move is to N0. So Player 2 moves to N0. Then Player 1 is at N0. From N0 → N4 (terminal). So Player 1 moves to N4. Now the game ends — Player 2 has no move → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n\nYes — so Player 1 wins.\n\nIn contrast, if Player 1 moves to N9, Player 2 can immediately move to any of the terminal nodes (N6, N11, N10, N12) and win.\n\nTherefore, N1 → N9 leads to Player 2 winning.\n\nOnly N1 → N2 leads to Player 1 winning.\n\nThus, Player 1 should choose N2 to maximize outcome.\n\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, Player 1 