ve.\n\nLet’s analyze each possible move from N3:\n\nOption 1: Move to N1\nFrom N1, possible moves are:\n- N6 → N0 (terminal)\n- N5 → N0, N7, N9\n- N2 → terminal\n\nSo from N1, the next player (Player 2) can choose:\n- N6 → leads to N0 (terminal) → Player 1 wins\n- N5 → leads to N0, N7, or N9 — all of which eventually end in terminal nodes\n- N2 → terminal → Player 1 wins immediately\n\nSo from N1, Player 2 can choose any of these, but all lead to terminal nodes or to positions from which Player 1 can eventually win. However, we must consider that if Player 2 moves to N5, they can go to N7 → N8 (terminal), or N9 (terminal), or N0 (terminal). So all paths from N5 lead to terminal nodes. So no matter what, Player 2 will eventually be forced into a terminal node after their move, and Player 1 will then have no legal move — no, wait: actually, if Player 2 moves to a terminal node, then Player 1 gets to move from there — but terminal nodes have no outgoing edges, so Player 1 cannot move. Therefore, if Player 2 moves to a terminal node, Player 1 loses.\n\nWait — correction: The rule is: \"A player with no legal move loses.\" So if a player is at a terminal node, they have no legal move, so they lose.\n\nTherefore, if Player 1 moves to a terminal node, the game ends immediately — Player 2 has no move and loses.\n\nBut in our case, Player 1 moves first to N1 or N4.\n\nSo if Player 1 moves to N1, then Player 2 moves from N1. From N1, Player 2 can:\n- Move to N6 → then to N0 (terminal) → Player 1 now has no move → Player 1 loses\n- Move to N5 → from N5, Player 1 can move to N0, N7, or N9 — all of which are terminal → so Player 1 can move to a terminal node, forcing Player 2 to lose\n- Move to N2 → terminal → Player 2 moves to terminal → Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses\n\nSo if Player 2 chooses N6 or N2, Player 1 loses. If Player 2 chooses N5, then Player 1 can respond by moving to N0, N7, or N9 — all terminal — and then Player 2 has no move and loses.\n\nTherefore, from 