yer 1 has no move → Player 1 loses immediately.\n   - Therefore, Player 2 can win from N11 by choosing N3.\n\nThus, moving to N8 leads to an immediate win (Player 1 wins), while moving to N11 gives Player 2 a winning strategy.\n\nTherefore, the optimal move is to N8.\n\nNote: The game ends when a player cannot move. Since N8 is terminal, after Player 1 moves to N8, Player 2 cannot move → Player 2 loses → Player 1 wins.\n\nThis is the best outcome: a guaranteed win.\n\n</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>", "episode_score": 1.0, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<reason>\nWe are at node N6 as Player 1. The legal moves from N6 are to N8 and N11.\n\nWe analyze each move in terms of game outcome, considering that the player who cannot move loses. This is a variant of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) vertex geography game, where the goal is to force the opponent into a position with no legal moves.\n\nFirst, evaluate each destination:\n\n1. Move to N8:\n   - N8 → terminal (no outgoing edges)\n   - So after Player 1 moves to N8, it's Player 2's turn.\n   - From N8, no moves are possible → Player 2 cannot move → Player 2 loses.\n   - Therefore, Player 1 wins immediately.\n\n2. Move to N11:\n   - From N11, the moves are to N3, N4, N1.\n   - So Player 2 can choose one of these.\n     - N3 → terminal → if Player 2 moves to N3, then Player 1 has no move (since N3 has no outgoing edges), so Player 1 loses.\n     - N4 → leads to N7 and N9 (both have outgoing edges), so Player 1 can continue.\n     - N1 → leads to N10, N9, N2 (all of which eventually lead to terminal nodes).\n   - So from N11, Player 2 has multiple options, and can potentially force a win.\n   - Specifically, if Player 2 moves to N3, then Player 1 has no move → Player 1 loses immediately.\n   - Therefore, Player 2 can win from N11 by choosing N3.\n\nThus, moving to N8 leads to an immediate win (Player 1 wins), while moving to N11 gives Player 2 a winning strategy.\n\nTherefore, the optimal move is to N8.\n\nNote: T