oves.\n\n- N13 → N7 → N10 → terminal → yes! N10 → terminal\n\n- N13 → N7 → N12 → terminal → yes!\n\nSo if we go to N7, then opponent must go to either N10 or N12 — both terminal.\n\nSo the opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nWait — again, opponent makes the move to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nSo no matter what, if we go to N7, opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nSimilarly, if we go to N2:\n\nN13 → N2 → N1 → from N1, opponent can go to N8 → N5 → terminal → opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nOr N1 → N6 → terminal → opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nOr N1 → N9 → N3 → N11 → N4 → terminal → opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nSo in all cases, after our move, opponent moves to terminal → opponent wins.\n\nSo both options lead to opponent winning?\n\nBut that suggests we lose no matter what.\n\nBut perhaps we can find a path where we force the opponent into a position where they have no move — that is, a dead end.\n\nWait — but all terminal nodes are dead ends. So the game ends when a player moves to a terminal node.\n\nTherefore, the player who moves to a terminal node wins.\n\nSo we want to move to a node from which the opponent", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>\nWe are playing Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) Vertex Geography, where players alternate moving a token along a directed edge. The player who cannot make a move loses. We are Player 1, starting at node N13.\n\nOur goal is to choose a move that maximizes our final game outcome — that is, we want to force a win, or at minimum, avoid losing, by considering the structure of the graph and the game tree.\n\nCurrent node: N13  \nFrom N13, legal moves are: N2 and N7.\n\nWe analyze each option:\n\n---\n\n**Option 1: Move to N2**\n\nFrom N2 → N1  \nFrom N1 → N8, N6, N9\n\nSo the path becomes: N13 → N2 → N1\n\nFrom N1:\n- N1 → N8 → N5 → terminal (win for opponent if they go to terminal)\n- N1 → N6 → terminal\n- N1 → N9 → N3 → N11 → N4 → terminal\n  or