ou lose.\n\nSo, for any node with no outgoing edges: it's a losing position (you lose immediately).\n\nNow, for any node with outgoing edges:  \n- If *all* moves lead to a losing position for the opponent, then this is a winning position (you can move to a losing position for them).  \n- If *any* move leads to a winning position for the opponent, then this is a losing position (because no matter what you do, the opponent can win).\n\nLet’s proceed from terminal nodes backward.\n\nWe will compute the status of each node.\n\n---\n\n**Terminal nodes:**\n- N1: terminal → losing (no moves)\n- N5: terminal → losing\n- N8: terminal → losing\n- N10: terminal → losing\n- N13: terminal → losing\n\nSo any node that leads directly to these is critical.\n\nNow consider nodes with one or more edges:\n\n---\n\n**N2 → N13**  \nN13 is terminal → losing for next player.  \nSo from N2, you move to N13, opponent loses → N2 is a winning position.\n\n**N0 → N10**  \nN10 is terminal → opponent loses → N0 is winning.\n\n**N7 → N5**  \nN5 is terminal → opponent loses → N7 is winning.\n\n**N4 → N2, N0**  \n- N2: winning (as above)  \n- N0: winning  \nSo both moves lead to winning positions for opponent → from N4, any move puts opponent in a winning position → N4 is **losing**\n\n**N3 → N7, N1**  \n- N7 → terminal (winning for opponent) → opponent wins  \n- N1 → terminal → opponent loses  \nSo one move (to N1) leads to opponent losing → so N3 is **winning**\n\n**N11 → N9, N0, N6**  \nWe need to evaluate N9, N0, N6.\n\n- N0 → terminal → opponent loses → so N0 is a winning move for current player  \n- N6 → ?  \n- N9 → ?\n\nSo if any of N9, N6 leads to opponent losing, then N11 is winning.\n\nLet’s analyze N6:\n\n**N6 → N9, N3, N4**\n\n- N9 → ?  \n- N3 → winning (as above)  \n- N4 → we just found: N4 is losing (all moves lead to opponent winning)\n\nSo from N6:\n- Move to N3 → opponent wins (N3 is winning for opponent)  \n- Move to N4 → opponent is in losing position → good!  \n- Move to N9 → de