rminal node, the game ends, and the opponent has no move — so the player who just moved wins.\n\nWait — clarify:\n\n- The player who moves to a terminal node wins, because the opponent has no legal move.\n\nYes — that’s standard in such games: if you move to a terminal node, the game ends and the opponent cannot move — so you win.\n\nSo if from N8, opponent moves to N13 → game ends → opponent loses → we win.\n\nIf opponent moves to N5 → then it's our turn at N5.\n\nSo opponent will choose between:\n- Move to N13 → lose immediately → bad for opponent\n- Move to N5 → then we get to play from N5\n\nSo opponent will prefer to go to N5 to avoid losing immediately.\n\nSo opponent will move to N5.\n\nNow we are at N5.\n\nFrom N5 → can go to N0, N1, or N12.\n\n- N1 → terminal → game ends → opponent loses → we win\n- N0 → N2 → terminal → opponent loses → we win\n- N12 → N11 → N4 or N10\n\nSo from N5, we have multiple winning moves.\n\nFor example:\n- Move to N1 → terminal → opponent loses → we win\n- Move to N0 → N2 → terminal → opponent loses → we win\n- Move to N12 → then opponent at N11 → from N11 → N4 or N10 → both terminal → so opponent moves to terminal → then we win\n\nSo from N5, all moves lead to opponent being forced to move to a terminal node — thus we win.\n\nTherefore, if we go to N8 → opponent moves to N5 → we then move to N1 (or N0) → opponent loses → we win.\n\nEven if opponent goes to N13, they lose immediately — so they will avoid it.\n\nSo opponent will go to N5 → we win.\n\nTherefore, moving to N8 leads to a win.\n\nNow compare:\n\n- N6 → leads to opponent winning (we lose)\n- N8 → leads to us winning\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N8.\n\nAlso, note that N8 leads to a forced win due to the structure of the graph.\n\nThus, choosing N8 maximizes our outcome.\n\n</reason>\n<answer>N8</answer>"}]
