n reverse order (from leaves toward root).\n\n#### N4 → N7, N3\n- Both N7 and N3 are terminal.\n- So from N4, you can move to terminal → opponent cannot move → you win.\n→ N4 is a **winning** position.\n\n#### N3 → terminal → no outgoing → so if you are at N3, you lose.\n→ N3 is **losing** (but only if it's your turn)\n\nWait: if you are at N3, you cannot move → you lose.\n\nSo:\n- N3 → losing position (no move)\n\nBut from N4, you can move to N3 or N7 — both terminal → so you can win by moving to terminal.\n\nSo N4 is **winning**.\n\n#### N7 → terminal → losing (no move)\n\n#### N5 → terminal → losing\n\n#### N1 → terminal → losing\n\n#### N9 → N2, N5\n- From N9, you can go to N2 or N5.\n- N5 is terminal → so you can move to terminal → opponent loses → you win.\n- But if you go to N2, opponent can then move from N2 to N1 (terminal), and you lose.\n\nBut since you can move to terminal (N5), you can win immediately.\n\nSo from N9, you can choose to go to N5 → opponent loses → so N9 is a **winning** position.\n\n#### N2 → N1\n- From N2, you can only go to N1 (terminal).\n- So you move to terminal → opponent loses → you win.\n→ N2 is a **winning** position.\n\n#### N6 → N9, N4\n- From N6, you can go to N9 or N4.\n- N4 → leads to terminal (N7 or N3) → so from N4, you can win immediately.\n", "episode_score": -1.5, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>\nWe are playing Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) Vertex Geography, where players alternate moving a token along a directed edge. The player who cannot make a move loses. We start at node N0, and we are Player 1. Our goal is to choose a move that maximizes our chances of winning — that is, to force the opponent into a losing position, considering all future moves and responses.\n\nLet’s analyze the structure of the graph and the terminal nodes:\n\n- Terminal nodes (no outgoing edges): N1, N3, N5, N7, N9 (note: N9 has outgoing edges, so it's not terminal — correction needed).\nWait: let's clarify what \"terminal\" means.\n\nA terminal node 