ition for the opponent.\n\nA position is a **winning position** if there exists at least one move that leads to a losing position for the opponent.\n\nWe work backwards from terminal nodes.\n\nTerminal nodes (no outgoing edges):  \n- N0, N1, N6, N8, N9 (after N9 → N0), N11, N13\n\nWait: N9 → N0, so N9 is not terminal — only N0 is terminal.\n\nSo terminal nodes are:\n- N0, N1, N6, N8, N11, N13\n\nAny node that leads to a terminal node on the next move is a winning move.\n\nLet’s define a node as **winning** if the current player can force a win from it.\n\nA node is **losing** if all moves from it lead to a winning position for the opponent.\n\nWe work backwards from terminal nodes.\n\nTerminal nodes: no outgoing edges → the player whose turn it is at a terminal node has no move → they lose.\n\nBut if a player moves to a terminal node, the opponent has no move and thus loses.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Any node that has an edge to a terminal node is a **winning** node (because the player can move to a terminal node and win).\n\nLet’s classify nodes based on whether they are winning or losing.\n\nWe’ll go from leaves inward.\n\n---\n\n**Terminal nodes**: N0, N1, N6, N8, N11, N13 → if you move to any of these, opponent loses → so the player who moves to them wins.\n\nSo any node with a direct edge to a terminal node is a **winning** node (because you can move to terminal and win).\n\nNow, nodes with only edges to non-terminal nodes — we need to check if any of those lead to terminal nodes.\n\nLet’s go node by node.\n\n---\n\nStart with N13 → terminal → no outgoing → so if you move to N13, opponent loses → so any node with edge to N13 is winning.\n\nSimilarly:\n- N1"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome.\n\nAnalyze the graph carefully. Consider future moves and the oppo